WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:02.960
If you buy a clock like this,
one that says "radio controlled",

00:00:02.960 --> 00:00:05.840
you just put a battery in, and it
sets itself to the correct time.

00:00:05.840 --> 00:00:09.120
And it stays there, automatically,
for a year or more.

00:00:09.120 --> 00:00:11.520
So it can't be receiving
signals from satellites,

00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:12.800
there's no wifi connection,

00:00:12.800 --> 00:00:15.680
there's nowhere near enough power
in here for that.

00:00:15.680 --> 00:00:18.240
Instead, it uses
much older technology.

00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.120
Here at the radio transmission
station in Anthorn,

00:00:21.120 --> 00:00:25.680
the National Physical Laboratory, NPL,
broadcasts a time signal to the UK

00:00:25.680 --> 00:00:26.880
in a simple enough format that

00:00:26.880 --> 00:00:29.520
the cheap electronics inside
this clock can understand it,

00:00:29.520 --> 00:00:33.520
and run accurately for over a year
on a single double-A battery.

00:00:33.520 --> 00:00:37.200
And at NPL's base in London,
I asked their team how it all works.

00:00:37.200 --> 00:00:42.560
- What we have in Anthorn are some
very high-end cesium atomic clocks,

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.240
like the ones we have down here.

00:00:44.240 --> 00:00:49.440
We broadcast from there,
we monitor the signals at NPL

00:00:49.440 --> 00:00:55.280
and accordingly put out steers to those
clocks to correct for any any change.

00:00:56.000 --> 00:00:58.400
It's a very low frequency,
60 kilohertz,

00:00:58.400 --> 00:01:02.720
it has very low susceptibility
to atmospheric effects.

00:01:02.720 --> 00:01:08.640
We steer from NPL based on measuring
what Anthorn is broadcasting

00:01:08.640 --> 00:01:10.400
at the level of nanoseconds.

00:01:10.400 --> 00:01:14.400
That is a nanosecond, the distance light
travels in one nanosecond, 30 centimeters.

00:01:15.440 --> 00:01:16.800
All that is taken into account.

00:01:16.800 --> 00:01:20.960
- So that seemed simple enough.
The official timescale is kept in London.

00:01:20.960 --> 00:01:23.360
I wasn't allowed anywhere near
the official atomic clocks,

00:01:23.360 --> 00:01:26.640
I don't even know if they're actually
at NPL's headquarters or not,

00:01:26.640 --> 00:01:28.800
they might be somewhere else.
They told me nothing!

00:01:28.800 --> 00:01:32.320
But up north here, this transmitter
has separate atomic clocks,

00:01:32.320 --> 00:01:34.160
about four hundred kilometres
away from the base.

00:01:34.160 --> 00:01:37.280
Or 1.4 light-milli-seconds away.

00:01:37.280 --> 00:01:40.800
There's a light-speed delay, a latency,
of about a thousandth of a second

00:01:40.800 --> 00:01:43.120
between the transmitter here
and the base in London.

00:01:43.120 --> 00:01:46.320
Because the team at NPL know exactly
what that delay should be,

00:01:46.320 --> 00:01:48.880
they can issue corrections if
these clocks start to drift,

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:51.440
even by nanoseconds,
billionths of seconds.

00:01:51.440 --> 00:01:53.440
Officially, the accuracy is much
lower than that, of course,

00:01:53.440 --> 00:01:55.600
partly because they keep
a very wide safety margin,

00:01:55.600 --> 00:02:00.000
and partly because I can introduce
a one-nanosecond drift to this clock

00:02:00.000 --> 00:02:01.280
by doing that.

00:02:01.280 --> 00:02:06.560
- What is really important when you're
transferring time is not about the latency,

00:02:06.560 --> 00:02:08.400
but understanding the latency.

00:02:08.400 --> 00:02:10.560
So you can take that out of the equation.

00:02:10.560 --> 00:02:14.640
From the NPL, we disseminate to
the UK over radio broadcast.

00:02:14.640 --> 00:02:17.920
Essentially that offers
the entire UK mainland

00:02:18.560 --> 00:02:21.760
several milliseconds of
traceability to UTC.

00:02:21.760 --> 00:02:24.720
We also have a fibre-delivered service

00:02:24.720 --> 00:02:27.440
for regulatory compliance
in the finance sector,

00:02:27.440 --> 00:02:29.280
guaranteed at the microsecond.

00:02:29.280 --> 00:02:34.000
And that's fine, I can understand all that.
But then that leads to another question:

00:02:34.000 --> 00:02:38.240
how does NPL know if their clocks
in London start to drift?

00:02:38.240 --> 00:02:40.160
Sure, they've got many of them,
they cross-check,

00:02:40.160 --> 00:02:42.880
and a "second" in the 21st century
is defined

00:02:42.880 --> 00:02:46.240
by the number of vibrations
of a cesium atom.

00:02:46.240 --> 00:02:47.680
But still, they can't be perfect.

00:02:47.680 --> 00:02:49.360
They're meant to show
the international standad,

00:02:49.360 --> 00:02:51.440
UTC, coordinated universal time,

00:02:51.440 --> 00:02:57.200
so what if NPL's official source of
timekeeping truth drifts from that standard?

00:02:57.200 --> 00:03:01.120
- UTC or coordinated universal time,
as the name suggests,

00:03:01.680 --> 00:03:06.880
is coordinated globally, contributing
data from multiple atomic clocks

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:10.400
to generate what is
the global timescale, UTC.

00:03:11.280 --> 00:03:16.160
What we have here at NPL is
the UK's timescale, UTC(NPL).

00:03:16.720 --> 00:03:18.960
We have a whole suite of atomic clocks,

00:03:19.600 --> 00:03:23.280
cesium clocks, hydrogen masers,
and a cesium fountain.

00:03:23.280 --> 00:03:26.320
The Bureau of Weights and Measures
based in France

00:03:26.320 --> 00:03:28.880
take the data from the
clocks around the globe,

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:33.920
and inform each of the national labs
how offset it is from UTC.

00:03:33.920 --> 00:03:36.800
- And that's where the buck stops.
In Paris.

00:03:36.800 --> 00:03:40.080
Unless you start getting into
physics and relativity and light cones,

00:03:40.080 --> 00:03:41.120
and that's way beyond me.

00:03:41.680 --> 00:03:42.480
Why does it matter?

00:03:42.480 --> 00:03:44.960
Well, first of all, finance
and high-frequency trading.

00:03:44.960 --> 00:03:45.840
But also: science.

00:03:46.400 --> 00:03:50.080
Things like the Square Kilometre Array,
a planet-wide array of radio telescopes

00:03:50.080 --> 00:03:52.000
that's being planned and built now.

00:03:52.000 --> 00:03:55.600
When they're listening for incredibly
faint radio waves from space,

00:03:55.600 --> 00:03:58.640
all the telescopes around the globe
have to be synchronised

00:03:58.640 --> 00:04:02.000
with that level of precision, or the
whole experiment could fall apart.

00:04:02.000 --> 00:04:04.240
So then, I had a final question:

00:04:04.240 --> 00:04:07.120
when accurate timecode is being
sent from so many places,

00:04:07.120 --> 00:04:09.600
when the public can get time
that's more than accurate enough

00:04:09.600 --> 00:04:12.800
for almost every purpose
from navigation satellites in space:

00:04:13.520 --> 00:04:15.760
why is this signal still important?

00:04:15.760 --> 00:04:18.560
And the team from NPL had a rather serious answer.

00:04:18.560 --> 00:04:20.800
- Time really is an invisible utility.

00:04:20.800 --> 00:04:23.040
It underpins our digital infrastructure,

00:04:23.040 --> 00:04:26.560
whether it's the synchronization
of the energy grid,

00:04:26.560 --> 00:04:29.280
or the telecom networks, or finance.

00:04:29.280 --> 00:04:32.880
We are so dependent on GPS
and other constellations.

00:04:32.880 --> 00:04:36.960
One of the problems we face is
those signals are easily disrupted.

00:04:36.960 --> 00:04:39.600
It's the equivalent of
a light bulb on the moon.

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:41.440
One of the things we're
doing at the NPL

00:04:41.440 --> 00:04:45.440
is to ensure that the UK is
resilient for the future.

00:04:46.402 --> 00:04:49.383
- Standing by the ocean
with a domestic appliance.

00:04:49.383 --> 00:04:51.963
The weird thing is,
it's not the first time.

